[2025-04-14 21:29:16,724][DEBUG][org.apache.http.wire] http-outgoing-2 << ";noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Jason Kao&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; discusses how to effectively combine Resource Control Policies (RCPs) and Service Control Policies (SCPs), including an example of how to migrate from an SCP to RCP. Recommendations: Use RCPs when you[0xe2][0x80][0x99]re protecting resources in S3, STS, KMS, SQS, or Secrets Manager, are enforcing consistent security standards for resources, are running out of attachment room or space in SCPs, or you want to use NotResource in your policy. Use SCPs if you need to restrict a service not covered by RCPs or you want to use NotAction.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Shz-FkwPqTW6hJuIjzacjKzHa4CNJ_g8sJAnX9ZRlK-lphCKR-oT677wJ_h8WMHJjnWe2_5jX5LlLUQOfrWljjKvRhlJy0xHyUuDBDEiHTjyJw3eXNwZyvDyZ1IihudN2XeqR052H9b-Y2zIvqDIZXp6vIvAni4AbwqbLmoecyJ8KPSsmcrum9xZbfwRI1itClxfpangk_uIzCx9O41lbq-ycKSDeUHK3H4b_lJGCYtJD9YVyRseCSP84PJA0CD0IGZ1fG29O8l7RVVfoqRBZ1ih1ANTkzp0J5WQYPjfWpUhj3V2nfknPSh1nv8C2DdHO6GEhhRgiHFACuHOxQoVwXZz872SbMzpFBoR9cBhEsuJjMNMnaa2SZ5L3gSD0LcpwEJBEwvuhXR9SsJJ2M7PCEQQ/4f5/Bfq3makRTpKF0Npi0qUnIA/h18/h001.qPsSjzU230CSY6LIWRFVDGL_uJlkpSXvCsJNSZ3vUQI&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;PowerUserAccess vs. AdministratorAccess from an attacker&amp;#39;s perspective&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;OffensAI[0xe2][0x80][0x99]s &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;a class=&quot;link&quot; href=&quot;https://link.mail.beehiiv.com/ss/c/u001.-QQnKmN9995ElcpR565Sh-SDvgyu6m58HhCZ9db8DGIfJsTOGlv-OfESNH6tS1QQmfqJ1vtG6nuD-AoI7CUsU3Rsq0TTrTet6F21wSAg12mpKhnPVQboe4uv64kZi8CfqV2oOKZs0fbKRg8Gh36_oM3sxCMOhWLr4P7lBycQ0jpFKj_VkPww5wGOOpIE5x3w7_RmI78RDjGGaMCQFVmjpaERoKip8HXF2HR5ApsWw3KerlWHxCN7wZfdpWbE0xSWbkvU5Sw4O7E5cBEVBxlzZzwkBamRqkiSCBaX_C8qRO3iiEgATPRCggkuwAcO51ijuWfOBSUbrIQWlB1hf7uHUJk6LtzvDVjzzOGT2M141Ew/4f5/Bfq3makRTpKF0Npi0qUnIA/h19/h001.3noiKiD8EDW-Hf243Ux_Lcojp1NZ0oTI1sGXNVp-TFI&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;&lt;span&gt;Eduard Agavriloae&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; describes how the AWS managed policy &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;PowerUserAccess&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; can be as dangerous as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;AdministratorAccess&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; in complex AWS environments. For example, through &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;PowerUserAccess&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;, attackers can escalate privileges by modifying and invoking a Lambda function with inappropriate IAM permissions, using SSM SendCommand to exfiltrate access credentials from an EC2 instance with an overly permissive role, or altering a CloudFormation template with &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;AdministratorAccess&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt; to include a privilege escalation vector.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class=&quot;dd&quot; align=&quot;left&quot; style=&quot;padding:0px 15px;text-align:left;word-break:break-word;&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;line-height:24px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&